**Analysis of the problem:**

The problem asks: for each prime $p \leq N$, can we choose a residue class $a_p \pmod{p}$ such that every integer $n \leq N$ lies in at least 10 of these classes?

**Counting obstruction (lower bound on $N$):** Each residue class $a_p \pmod{p}$ covers roughly $N/p$ integers in $[1,N]$. The total coverage is $\sum_{p \leq N} N/p \approx N \ln \ln N$ (Mertens' theorem). For every integer to be covered at least 10 times, we need total coverage $\geq 10N$, i.e., $\ln \ln N \gtrsim 10$, requiring $N \geq e^{e^{10}} \approx 10^{9566}$. For $N$ below this threshold, the pigeonhole principle makes it **impossible**.

**Probabilistic method attempt (large $N$):** Choose each $a_p$ uniformly at random. For a fixed $n$, the coverage $X_n = \sum_{p \leq N} \mathbf{1}[n \equiv a_p \pmod{p}]$ has mean $\mu = \sum_{p \leq N} 1/p \approx \ln \ln N$. By Chernoff, $P(X_n < 10) \leq e^{-c\mu}$ for some $c > 0$ when $\mu$ is large. A union bound over all $n \leq N$ requires $N \cdot e^{-c \ln \ln N} < 1$, i.e., $N/(\ln N)^c < 1$. But $N/(\ln N)^c \to \infty$ for any fixed $c$, so **the union bound fails** — the naive probabilistic method cannot establish the result.

**Why this is open:** The failure of the union bound is precisely what makes this a genuine open problem. The correlations between coverage events for different $n$ are subtle, and no known technique (Lovász Local Lemma, alteration methods, semi-random methods, etc.) has been shown to overcome this barrier. Ben Green poses this as Open Problem 45 precisely because it sits in a regime where standard tools break down.

This is a well-known open problem in combinatorial number theory (Erdős problem 689 / Green's Problem 45). No proof or disproof is currently known for the regime $N \geq e^{e^{10}}$.

